1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,488
The brush finds a stone, shaped on both faces to a point.

2
00:00:04,488 --> 00:00:05,725
Somebody made this.

3
00:00:05,725 --> 00:00:13,324
Not a factory, not a workshop — a person, sitting down, striking one rock with another until it did what they wanted.

4
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:21,670
This is the oldest technology in India, and it ran for hundreds of thousands of years.

5
00:00:35,167 --> 00:00:38,313
For this episode there is one source and one only.

6
00:00:38,313 --> 00:00:39,732
Nobody wrote anything.

7
00:00:39,732 --> 00:00:41,583
Nobody carved an inscription.

8
00:00:41,583 --> 00:00:45,955
Everything we know, we know because it was dug out of the ground.

9
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:56,934
The Stone Age is split into three, and the split is about tools.

10
00:00:56,934 --> 00:01:05,262
First, the Palaeolithic, the old stone age: big tools struck from stone cores — hand axes, cleavers, choppers.

11
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:15,362
People hunted and gathered, moved with the seasons, and lived in caves and open camps.

12
00:01:21,133 --> 00:01:22,134
And they painted.

13
00:01:22,134 --> 00:01:28,080
At Bhimbetka in Madhya Pradesh there are hundreds of rock shelters whose walls carry paintings of animals,

14
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:31,492
hunts and dances, made across a very long span of time.

15
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:41,841
It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and it is the closest thing we have to the imagination of the first Indians.

16
00:01:46,467 --> 00:01:48,836
Then the Mesolithic, the middle stone age.

17
00:01:48,836 --> 00:01:51,427
The tools get small — microliths, tiny blades,

18
00:01:51,427 --> 00:01:56,797
often set in a row along a handle of wood or bone to make a cutting edge or a barbed point.

19
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:10,679
Smaller tools meant finer work and better hunting, and some groups began to keep animals.

20
00:02:15,267 --> 00:02:21,677
Then the Neolithic, the new stone age, and the change that matters more than any other in human history.

21
00:02:21,677 --> 00:02:23,021
People began to farm.

22
00:02:23,021 --> 00:02:26,317
Tools are now ground and polished instead of chipped.

23
00:02:26,317 --> 00:02:29,792
Pottery appears, and storage, and permanent houses.

24
00:02:36,467 --> 00:02:46,206
At Mehrgarh, in the Bolan valley, there is evidence of wheat and barley farming and herded animals from about 7000 BCE. At Burzahom in the Kashmir valley,

25
00:02:46,206 --> 00:02:48,845
people dug pit dwellings into the ground.

26
00:02:48,845 --> 00:02:53,167
And at Koldihwa in the Belan valley, there is evidence of rice.

27
00:02:56,533 --> 00:02:58,497
How old is the art at Bhimbetka?

28
00:02:58,497 --> 00:03:03,439
Some scholars argue the oldest layers reach back into the Upper Palaeolithic.

29
00:03:07,533 --> 00:03:11,008
Others hold that most of what survives is Mesolithic or later,

30
00:03:11,008 --> 00:03:15,806
because paintings were made again and again on the same walls over thousands of years.

31
00:03:15,806 --> 00:03:20,849
Dating paint on rock is genuinely hard, and the honest answer is a range, not a date.

32
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:30,431
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

33
00:03:30,431 --> 00:03:32,463
Three ages, three words.

34
00:03:32,463 --> 00:03:35,200
Big stone, small stone, seed.

35
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:42,086
Dig Quiz.

36
00:03:42,086 --> 00:03:43,974
Pause and name the age.

37
00:03:43,974 --> 00:03:49,558
First: a large pointed tool, worked on both faces, struck from a core.

38
00:03:49,558 --> 00:03:50,959
Palaeolithic.

39
00:03:54,967 --> 00:03:59,888
Second: blades the size of a fingernail, set in a row along a handle.

40
00:03:59,888 --> 00:04:01,032
Mesolithic.

41
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:14,997
Third: a ground and polished axe, found beside stored grain.

42
00:04:14,997 --> 00:04:15,772
Neolithic.

43
00:04:15,772 --> 00:04:21,496
In Mains, a classic question asks why the shift to farming mattered so much.

44
00:04:24,867 --> 00:04:30,918
The answer is not better food — it is settlement, surplus, storage, and everything those make possible.

45
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:42,672
A hand axe takes its place in the gallery: a tool that stayed useful for longer than everything that came after it put together.

46
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:50,719
Next gallery: the first metal, and the villages that used it.

47
00:04:50,719 --> 00:04:54,592
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

